}
\details{
\strong{The grade.} In Rabe-Hesketh and Skrondal's essay data, each essay has
grades from five experts on 8 levels. \code{grade} is the grade of one of them
(grader 2), collapsed in pairs: 1-2 = 1, 3-4 = 2, 5-6 = 3 and 7-8 = 4.
The four levels hold 68, 48, 40 and 42 essays. The grade is ordinal: the
distances between its levels have no fixed size.

\strong{Text features.} \code{wordlength} is the average word length in characters,
\code{sqrtwords} the square root of the number of words, \code{commas} the number of
commas per 100 words, \code{errors} the percentage of words with a spelling
error (spelling only, not grammar), \code{prepos} the percentage of words that
are prepositions and \code{sentlength} the average sentence length in words.
No value is missing, and the values are unchanged in version 1.0.0.

\strong{Suggested analyses.}
\itemize{
\item A proportional-odds (ordered logit) model of \code{grade} on the text
features, for example with \code{MASS::polr()}: cumulative odds ratios and
cut-points.
\item The latent-response view: an ordered probit (\code{method = "probit"})
treats the grade as a continuous quality score cut at thresholds. Its
coefficients differ from the ordered logit's mainly by a scale factor.
\item For contrast, a linear regression that treats \code{grade} as a number, and
model building to choose among the six features.
}
